Options: save on the way out, and offer motion tilt and rumble.

Three changes to one screen, because they are one screen's worth of work.

**Saving.** Upstream calls `config_save` from exactly one place - `ball/main.c:602`, after
the main loop returns. On a desktop that is sound: quitting is how a game ends. A console
title is closed from the dashboard or killed, `main` never returns, and every volume,
camera and graphics choice made here is lost with it. Patch 0002 already covers the
player's name, which is a separate screen; this covers the rest. Leaving the options
screen is the narrowest place that catches all of them at once, and - unlike saving from
a close handler - it does not have to be right about how much time the system's kill
timer allows.

**Motion tilt**, which is off until it is switched on here. The backend is not a patch at
all: Neverball has had a tilt-sensor interface since it supported the Wiimote, upstream's
own Makefile picks one of three backends, and this port supplies a fourth in
`shim/nb_tilt_dualsense.c`. All that is needed here is the switch. It is shown only when
a pad with a usable IMU answered at start-up, because an option that is present and does
nothing reads as a broken port rather than as an absent sensor.

**Rumble**, from `common/haptics.h`, shared with every other port. Off is a real
preference - somebody plays late with the pad on a desk - and a game with no way to stop
it buzzing is a game they turn off.

Both toggles re-enter the screen so the label redraws, which is the pattern the video
options already use.

The two new tokens are appended to the enum rather than inserted, so every existing one
keeps its value.

diff --git a/ball/st_conf.c b/ball/st_conf.c
index dfe1b41..df037c2 100644
--- a/ball/st_conf.c
+++ b/ball/st_conf.c
@@ -33,6 +33,11 @@
 #include "st_ball.h"
 #include "st_shared.h"
 
+/* The port's own two options. `nb_tilt.h` is this title's (`shim/include`), `haptics.h` is
+   shared with every other port (`common/`); both are on the include path for this build. */
+#include "nb_tilt.h"
+#include "haptics.h"
+
 extern const char TITLE[];
 extern const char ICON[];
 
@@ -46,7 +51,11 @@ enum
     CONF_SOUND_VOLUME,
     CONF_MUSIC_VOLUME,
     CONF_PLAYER,
-    CONF_BALL
+    CONF_BALL,
+    /* The port's two. Appended rather than inserted, so every existing token keeps its value -
+       a saved GUI id or a translation catalogue keyed on these does not shift under them. */
+    CONF_TILT,
+    CONF_RUMBLE
 };
 
 static int mouse_id[11];
@@ -87,9 +96,32 @@ static int conf_action(int tok, int val)
     switch (tok)
     {
     case GUI_BACK:
+        /* **The settings are written here, not only at exit.** Upstream calls `config_save`
+         * from one place - `ball/main.c:602`, after the main loop returns - which is right on a
+         * desktop, where quitting is how a game ends. A console title is closed from the
+         * dashboard or killed, so `main` never returns and every volume, camera and graphics
+         * choice made in this screen is lost with it.
+         *
+         * Leaving the options screen is the narrowest place that covers all of them: it is
+         * where the player has finished changing things, it costs one file write on a screen
+         * reached rarely, and it needs nothing to be right about how long the system's kill
+         * timer gives us - which is why this is here rather than in a close handler. */
+        config_save();
         goto_state(&st_title);
         break;
 
+    case CONF_TILT:
+        /* Re-enters the screen so the toggle redraws with its new label. The backend re-centres
+           on the way on, so wherever the pad is being held becomes level. */
+        nb_tilt_set_enabled(val);
+        goto_state(&st_conf);
+        break;
+
+    case CONF_RUMBLE:
+        oops_haptics_set_enabled(val);
+        goto_state(&st_conf);
+        break;
+
     case CONF_VIDEO:
         goto_state(&st_video);
         break;
@@ -171,6 +203,19 @@ static int conf_gui(void)
 
         gui_space(id);
 
+        /* **The port's two, and the tilt one only when there is a pad that can do it.** An
+         * option that is present and does nothing is worse than an absent one: the player turns
+         * it on, nothing changes, and the conclusion is that the port is broken rather than that
+         * their controller has no usable motion sensor. See `shim/nb_tilt.h`. */
+        if (nb_tilt_available())
+            conf_toggle(id, _("Motion Tilt"), CONF_TILT,
+                        nb_tilt_enabled(), _("On"), 1, _("Off"), 0);
+
+        conf_toggle(id, _("Rumble"), CONF_RUMBLE,
+                    oops_haptics_enabled(), _("On"), 1, _("Off"), 0);
+
+        gui_space(id);
+
         name_id = conf_state(id, _("Player Name"), " ", CONF_PLAYER);
         ball_id = conf_state(id, _("Ball Model"), " ", CONF_BALL);
         lang_id = conf_state(id, _("Language"), " ", CONF_LANGUAGE);
